    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo.jpg" alt="Augustine of Hippo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Augustine_by_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe de Champaigne, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Vandal Kingdom was a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Alans planted on the Roman provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. In 429 the Vandals, under their new king Gaiseric, crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> put their number at 80,000, a figure some historians doubt, and Peter Heather estimates an army of 15,000 to 20,000. Procopius says they came at the invitation of Bonifacius, the military ruler of the region, though it has also been suggested that they sought safety after a Roman army attacked them in 422. Advancing eastward along the coast, they besieged the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> in 430. Inside, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Saint Augustine</a> and his priests prayed for relief; on 28 August 430, three months into the siege, the 75-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> died. After fourteen months the city fell and became the Vandals' first capital.</p><p>Peace came in 435 through a treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a> and Gaiseric, which gave the Vandals coastal Numidia and parts of Mauretania. Gaiseric broke it in 439, invaded Africa Proconsularis and laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, the most important city in North Africa. The city was captured without a fight: the Vandals entered while most of the inhabitants were at the races in the hippodrome. Gaiseric made it his capital and styled himself King of the Vandals and Alans. Most people in the kingdom considered themselves Roman; the Vandals were always a minority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/latin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latin.jpg" alt="Latin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rome_Colosseum_inscription_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wknight94, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> had been one of the richest parts of the Roman world: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> wrote that it fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for eight months of the year, and it was an important source of taxes as well as grain and oil. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> took over this export economy. They confiscated land held by senators and gave it to themselves, though those who lost estates kept their rights and the wealthy kept their wealth and power; a military landowning aristocracy replaced the largely absentee senatorial aristocracy and spent its wealth on luxurious town houses and religious buildings. The treaty of 442 seems to have ensured that grain shipments to Rome continued, though how long they went on is uncertain.</p><p>The state of the economy is hard to assess. The Vandals struck no gold coins, and taxes were raised chiefly to pay for an expanding army; from 477 they issued their own bronze and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> small change, leaving the high-denomination imperial money in place, which Andy Merrills reads as a reluctance to usurp the imperial prerogative. Trade continued at lower levels within a general fifth-century decline, but the quantities of African red slip ware of the Vandal period found around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> have led Merrills to question the picture of economic instability. The kings supported the building of educational institutions, and Richard Miles counts North Africa the home of many of the most innovative writers and natural scientists of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> West.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii.jpg" alt="Theodosius II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodosius_II_Louvre_Ma1036.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Vandal troops were fashioned on the Roman model, and sea power made the kingdom dangerous far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Gaiseric conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>. When he raided Sicily in 440 the Western Empire was too occupied with war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> to react, and an expedition sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> in 441 got no further than Sicily. For 35 years Gaiseric used his large fleet to loot the coasts of both empires, and in 455 he sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. When the emperor Majorian assembled a fleet at Cartagena in 460 to take Mauretania and march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, Gaiseric scoured the land and poisoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> in his path, then defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at Cartagena with his own fleet. In 468 both empires launched the most ambitious campaign ever mounted against the Vandal state; the sources speak of 1,113 ships and 100,000 men, though Heather suggests some 30,000 troops. At Cape Bon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> destroyed the Western fleet and part of the Eastern fleet with fire ships.</p><p>After Gaiseric's death the kingdom lost ground to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>: the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altava">Altava</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masties">Masties</a>'s kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a> in 483–484, Guenfan's Frexenses south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dougga">Thugga</a> in 510 and Cabaon's Laguatan, who took Oea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabratha">Sabratha</a> and defeated Thrasamund's expedition of 523. Gunthamund lost large parts of Sicily to Theodoric's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>. In 533 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> beat the Vandals at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ad-decimum">Battle of Ad Decimum</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tricamarum">Battle of Tricamarum</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gelimer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gelimer.jpg" alt="Gelimer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%BCnze_50_Denari_-_Gelimer_-_K%C3%B6nig_der_Vandalen_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The administration of the kingdom closely resembled the Roman provincial administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: it was staffed by local Africans, and its currency and taxation were adaptations of Roman models. Gaiseric styled himself King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Alans and promulgated a law of succession by which the oldest male of the royal house was to succeed; it carried the crown to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huneric">Huneric</a> in 477, then to Huneric's cousin Gunthamund, to Thrasamund, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>. Huneric murdered many rival members of the dynasty.</p><p>Religion was a matter of state. The Vandals were predominantly Arians and persecuted Nicene Christians, at first, Merrills and Miles argue, to seize the church's wealth. After 435 Gaiseric took the basilicas of three intransigent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and expelled them; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> four major churches were confiscated for the Arians, Nicene services were banned wherever Vandals settled, Bishop Quodvultdeus and many clergy were exiled and no replacements could be ordained, and Nicene laymen were excluded from office. Huneric went further after 482: Homoousian books were destroyed, almost 5,000 clergy exiled into the desert, and after a staged debate in 483 all Nicene churches were closed and their property given to the royal fisc or the Arian clergy. Gunthamund restored tolerance, Thrasamund renewed harsh measures against the hierarchy while courting the Romano-African lay elite, and Hilderic granted religious freedom, so that Chalcedonian synods met again.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Temple in Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_Modell_BW_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Berthold Werner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historians since Edward Gibbon have called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandal-conquest-of-north-africa">Vandal conquest of North Africa</a> the greatest single blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> had required only a small share of the empire's forces while supplying much of its taxes and the grain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; its loss, and the piracy that followed, made the destruction of the Vandal state a Roman priority and swallowed two great expeditions. The sack of Rome in 455, when Pope Leo the Great is said by Prosper of Aquitaine to have begged Gaiseric to be content with pillage, carried off countless valuables, among them the spoils of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> brought to Rome by Titus, and the empress Licinia Eudoxia and her daughters, one of whom, Eudocia, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huneric">Huneric</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. The episode fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>' name to senseless destruction in the word vandalism.</p><p>Yet the kingdom was also a working part of the late Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. After Ricimer's treaty and the perpetual peace Gaiseric concluded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 476, relations assumed a veneer of normality. Averil Cameron suggests the new rule may not have been unwelcome to Africans, since the old landowners were unpopular; African pottery still travelled across the sea, and writers and natural scientists flourished. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altava">Altava</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a> and the Dorsale rose in the space Vandal weakness left, and the best Vandal warriors ended as five cavalry regiments, the Vandali Iustiniani, on the Persian frontier of the empire that destroyed them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justinian-i.jpg" alt="Justinian I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_of_Justinianus_I_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_(Ravenna).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Vandal power declined from Gaiseric's death in 477. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, held in check in his reign, rose under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huneric">Huneric</a>'s strict religious laws; western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altava">Altava</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès Mountains</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masties">Masties</a>. Gunthamund lost much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and faced Berber raids that reached the coast; under Thrasamund the Frexenses founded the Kingdom of the Dorsale in 510, the Laguatan took Oea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabratha">Sabratha</a>, and the expedition sent against them in 523 ended in disaster. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a> left war to his nephew Hoamer, who was decisively beaten by the Frexes under Guenfan and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalas">Antalas</a>. The Arian faction of the royal family then revolted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> became king in 530, imprisoning Hilderic and Hoamer.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> declared war with the stated aim of restoring Hilderic, whom Gelimer executed in 533 as the Byzantine army approached. Gelimer's brother Tzazo had taken much of the army and navy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> against the rebel Godas, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> landed unopposed ten miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in the summer of 533. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ad-decimum">Battle of Ad Decimum</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> were prevailing until Gelimer's brother Ammatas and nephew Gibamund fell; Gelimer fled and Belisarius took Carthage. On 15 December 533 the Vandals broke at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tricamarum">Battle of Tricamarum</a> when Tzazo was killed, and in 534 Gelimer, besieged at Mount Pappua, surrendered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> became a Byzantine province; the remaining Vandals were shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> for the imperial army, and as a people they disappeared.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Vandal Kingdom, founded when Gaiseric led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Alans into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 439, held the grain lands of the empire and the western islands for 99 years, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and defeated two imperial fleets, and persecuted the Nicene church until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> destroyed it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandalic-war">Vandalic War</a> of 533–534.</em></p>

